from pathlib import Path
import json, html
root=Path('site/experiments/intelligence-explosion')
# Draft cards live in the private planning folder so unapproved text never ships.
cards_path=Path('planning/hub-cards.json')
cards=json.loads(cards_path.read_text()) if cards_path.exists() else []
esc=html.escape
approved={c['id']:c for c in cards if c['approved']}
LINKS={'garicano-imas':'https://www.siliconcontinent.com/p/are-we-approaching-an-intelligence','chalmers':'https://x.com/davidchalmers42/status/2120551211195642338'}
def ref(id,text):
 return f'<a href="{("#"+id) if id in approved else esc(LINKS[id],quote=True)}">{text}</a>'
def card(c):
 fields=[('What they claim.','claim'),('Kind of evidence.','kind'),('Questions.','questions'),('Their caveats.','caveats'),('How it fits.','fits'),('How sure.','tag')]
 return '<details id="'+esc(c['id'],quote=True)+'"><summary>'+esc(c['title'])+' <span>'+esc(c['who'])+'</span></summary>'+''.join('<p><strong>'+label+'</strong> '+esc(c[key])+'</p>' for label,key in fields if c.get(key))+'<p><a href="'+esc(c['link'],quote=True)+'">Read the original →</a></p></details>'
source=Path('site/experiments/spending-puzzle/index.html').read_text(); header=source[:source.index('<header class="head">')].replace('The spending puzzle ·','Is there an intelligence explosion? ·').replace('href="../intelligence-explosion/">Overview','href="../intelligence-explosion/" aria-current="page">Overview').replace('href="../spending-puzzle/" aria-current="page"','href="../spending-puzzle/"').replace('href="styles.css','href="../spending-puzzle/styles.css')
header=header.replace('<body>', '<body class="hub">')
opening='An intelligence explosion would mean AI improving AI research, with each improvement making the next one come sooner. Researchers, economists and philosophers now disagree about whether one has begun. This page gathers the work worth reading on that question, explains it in plain words, and says how it fits together. We update it as new work appears.'
# Replace inherited social metadata with the hub's own opening.
import re
header=re.sub(r'<meta (?:name="description"|property="og:(?:title|description)")[^>]*>','',header)
header=header.replace('</head>',f'<meta name="description" content="{esc(opening,quote=True)}"><meta property="og:title" content="Is there an intelligence explosion?"><meta property="og:description" content="{esc(opening,quote=True)}"></head>')
g=ref('garicano-imas','Garicano and Imas'); ch=ref('chalmers','Chalmers')
body='''<header class="head">Economics of AI · 14.11.2026</header><main class="sheet"><section id="chapter-1"><h1>Is there an intelligence explosion?</h1>
<div class="noted"><p>'''+opening+'''</p><aside class="note"><strong>OUR GUESS</strong> "Worth reading" is our judgement. Each entry says why it is here.</aside></div>
<h3>What an explosion would look like</h3>
<div class="noted"><p>Think of research as a curve. Along the bottom is the effort people pay for: money, staff and computers. Up the side is progress. In most fields the curve bends over. The easy ideas get found first, so each step forward costs more effort than the last. An intelligence explosion would bend the curve the other way. If AI does more of the research as it improves, each step makes the next one cheaper, and progress pulls away from the effort people pay for.</p><aside class="note"><strong>ESTABLISHED</strong> Both shapes come from standard models of research: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20180338">Bloom and others</a> for the first, <a href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w23928">Aghion, Jones and Jones</a> for the second. The lines are drawn by hand to show the shapes. They are not fitted to data.</aside></div>
<figure class="ie-curves"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 375" role="img" aria-labelledby="ie-curves-title"><title id="ie-curves-title">Two curves of progress against paid-for effort. One bends over, so each step costs more. The other bends upward, so each step makes the next cheaper.</title><path d="M50 20V330H505" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M50 330C150 165 300 115 500 95" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M50 330C250 320 400 240 470 20" fill="none" stroke="var(--red)" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="9 6"/><text x="60" y="80">Today: each step</text><text x="60" y="100">costs more than the last</text><text x="505" y="235" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--red)">If AI does the</text><text x="505" y="255" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--red)">research, each step</text><text x="505" y="275" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--red)">makes the next</text><text x="505" y="295" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--red)">one cheaper</text><text x="505" y="362" text-anchor="end">Effort people pay for →</text><text x="40" y="30" text-anchor="end" transform="rotate(-90 40 30)">Progress →</text></svg></figure>
<p>Much of the work on these pages was done by Claude, an AI made by Anthropic, the company whose research is measured here. Read with that in mind. <a href="https://github.com/ashishefe/lfe-research-machine">The code and the text are open</a>.</p></section>
<section><h2>Where things stand</h2><p>Last updated 14 November 2026.</p>'''
items=[("AI research at Anthropic has sped up.","After October 2025, Anthropic's AI models improved about 1.9 times as fast as before, on METR's measure.",'<a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-11-05-anthropic-ai-rd-acceleration-assessment/">METR</a>, 5 November 2026, on data Anthropic shared. Range: 1.5 to 2.8 times.'),("Nobody yet knows how much of that came from more money and staff, and how much from AI tools.","Progress can speed up because more goes in, or because more comes out of the same input. Only the second could be the start of an explosion. Anthropic spent far more, and each dollar bought less progress than before, as you would expect when ideas get harder to find. The question is whether progress still beat what the extra spending alone would predict. The only study so far that uses model-by-model scores finds that it did, in part. More inputs explain some of the speedup, and the rest looks like a one-time boost, though the authors say that estimate is uncertain. A one-time boost is not an explosion: an explosion needs the boost to keep growing. Telling these apart is the hard part, and it is what these pages work through.",'<a href="https://www.siliconcontinent.com/p/are-we-approaching-an-intelligence">Garicano and Imas</a>, 13 November 2026. On their figures, progress per dollar fell about 69%.'),("In AI research, the easy ideas are being used up.","Each step forward costs more than the last, as in many other fields.",'<a href="https://www.siliconcontinent.com/p/are-we-approaching-an-intelligence">Garicano and Imas</a> for AI. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20180338">Bloom and others</a> for other fields.'),("There is no clear sign yet of AI feeding its own research in a way that speeds up as AI improves.","That loop is what an explosion would need.",'<a href="https://www.siliconcontinent.com/p/are-we-approaching-an-intelligence">Garicano and Imas</a>, from their model.'),("It cannot be ruled out.","Daniel Kokotajlo, an author of the AI 2027 scenario, wrote that if METR is right, an explosion now looks likely in 2027. In the widest test in their appendix, Garicano and Imas put about a one-in-eleven chance on the condition for one already being met.",'<a href="https://x.com/DKokotajlo/status/2120204075803500595">Kokotajlo</a>, 10 November 2026, and the <a href="https://blog.aifutures.org/p/q35-timelines-update-looks-like-the">AI Futures post</a> he links. Garicano and Imas, <a href="https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/jz1zaj2fk741g2len97q9/Online-Appendix-to-Garicano-and-Imas-AI-ideas-getting-harder-to-find.pdf?rlkey=narofum32aiq105vrcwcx92vy&amp;dl=0">online appendix</a>, section F8.')]
for i,(a,b,c) in enumerate(items,1): body+=f'<div class="noted"><p>{i}. <strong>{esc(a)}</strong> {esc(b)}</p><aside class="note"><strong>CLAIMED</strong> {c}</aside></div>'
body+='''<div class="noted"><p></p><aside class="note"><strong>OUR GUESS</strong> This summary is ours. It will change as new work appears.</aside></div></section><section><h2>The questions inside the question</h2>
<h3>Is AI research speeding up?</h3><p>Yes, at Anthropic, by about 1.9 times on one measure, with a wide range. → <a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-11-05-anthropic-ai-rd-acceleration-assessment/">METR</a>.</p>
<h3>How much of the speedup is money, and how much is AI?</h3><p>Unsettled. The answer depends on what you assume would have happened anyway. → <a href="https://x.com/sebkrier/status/2119182834162340095">Krier</a> and <a href="https://x.com/michael_wiebe/status/2119207429393220041">Wiebe</a>; '''+g+'''; <a href="../spending-puzzle/">our spending puzzle</a>.</p>
<h3>Are ideas getting harder to find in AI?</h3><p>Yes, on the evidence so far, at a steady rate. → '''+g+'''.</p>
<h3>Is AI starting to do its own research?</h3><p>No clear sign yet of the self-feeding kind. David Chalmers asks a related question: the gains so far may be in the easier parts of research, such as coding and running experiments, and could grow fast there without reaching harder parts such as research judgement. → '''+g+'; '+ch+'''.</p>
<h3>What would an explosion need, and what would we see first?</h3><p>AI that gets better at the hard parts of research, not only the easy ones, which is Chalmers's question. And returns to research effort that stop falling and start to rise, which is the condition in Garicano and Imas's model. → '''+ch+'; '+g+'''.</p>
<h3>What would settle it?</h3><div class="noted"><p>Model-by-model scores, an itemised research budget, and how long spending takes to show up. → <a href="../spending-puzzle/#chapter-7">our spending puzzle, section 7</a>.</p><aside class="note"><strong>OUR GUESS</strong> The answers in this section are our reading of the work linked.</aside></div></section>
'''
end='<footer class="colophon">Built by Ashish Kulkarni with Claude and Codex. Claude is made by Anthropic. <a href="https://github.com/ashishefe/lfe-research-machine">Code and text are open.</a> Built to be argued with. Design craft learned from Keshav Jindal.</footer></main><script src="app.js"></script></body></html>'
record_open='<section><h2>The record</h2><p>Each entry: who and when, what they claim, the kind of evidence, and how it fits with the rest. Newest first.</p><div id="record">'
record_close='</div></section>'
(root/'index.html').write_text(header+body+((record_open+''.join(card(c) for c in approved.values())+record_close) if approved else '')+end)
# Preview lives outside the public site; absolute file URLs retain local assets and links.
preview=(header+body+record_open+''.join(card(c) for c in cards)+record_close+end).replace('<head>','<head><base href="'+root.resolve().as_uri()+'/">')
Path('dist').mkdir(exist_ok=True); Path('dist/hub-drafts-preview.html').write_text(preview)
